{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794571017848,"sessionID":"ses_e2b4abdd3ffe0ORlG2coZj9Q9y","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4c49a40002oLP9TqWqN9BwG4","sessionID":"ses_e2b4abdd3ffe0ORlG2coZj9Q9y","messageID":"msg_1d4c48120001epZEwtqYbNT8Ul","type":"tool","id":"call_f04c95dcf27441d19ede6bd1","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"L=specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4\njq -c '{keyFindings:(.keyFindings|length), openQuestions:(.openQuestions|length), resolved:(.resolvedQuestions|length), ruledOut:(.ruledOutDirections|length), metrics:.metrics}' \"$L/findings-registry.json\"; jq -r '.keyFindings[]?.text' \"$L/findings-registry.json\" | head -20"},"output":"{\"keyFindings\":17,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolved\":5,\"ruledOut\":8,\"metrics\":{\"iterationsCompleted\":3,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolvedQuestions\":5,\"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\":0,\"keyFindings\":17,\"rejectedPatterns\":0,\"suppressedCandidates\":0,\"observedIdeas\":0,\"promotedIdeas\":0,\"suppressedIdeas\":0,\"convergenceScore\":1,\"coverageBySources\":{\"code\":11,\"other\":12},\"completedPivots\":0,\"failedPivots\":0,\"pivotOverrides\":0}}\nA cold in-process path costs a median of 224.7 ms and a worst of 337.7 ms for import plus one handler call, and the whole fresh process finishes at a median of 239.4 ms and a worst of 443.0 ms. That worst case is 17.7 percent of the hook's 2,500 ms budget. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/iteration-001-summary.json]\nA warm handler call in a process that already loaded the modules costs a median of 76.5 ms and a worst of 93.6 ms across the ten prompts. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/warm.jsonl]\nThe cold in-process added cost is at parity with today's fallback, 224.7 ms against 238.5 ms, a difference of minus 13.8 ms at the median. The warm in-process call is 3.1 times cheaper than the Python spawn. A CLI process that already loaded its own modules pays the import only on its first degraded call, so the comparable number is the cold one. [INFERENCE: based on the cold, warm and fallback rows in the same summary file and the dynamic-import shape of the proposed call site]\nThe floors are unchanged at the built HEAD: typed recall 24 of 44, false offers 0 of 35, plain tune 23 of 45 and attached false offers 0 of 35. The capture took 8.96 s. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/floor-capture.json]\nThe handler module import is the largest fixed cost of the cold path: median 78.4 ms, worst 146.4 ms, which is 35 percent of the 224.7 ms cold median. The remaining 140.5 ms median is the first scoring call, including the prompt embedding. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/iteration-001-summary.json]\nToday's Python fallback scorer costs a median of 238.5 ms and a worst of 377.7 ms for the same ten prompts, measured as one spawn per prompt inside a warm node process. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/fallback.jsonl]\nA filter that trusts the handler's own command only when the prompt typed it keeps the typed readme command and adds no command to the nine untyped prompts: 1 of 1 typed command kept, 0 of 9 gained. That is the Q026 boundary, and it needs the filter to read the handler row rather than re-derive the command from the skill. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/shape-live.jsonl]\nThe handler's raw answer is the live answer on the cases rank 6 names: the plain readme prompt tops `sk-doc` where the fallback tops `system-spec-kit`, the create-agent prompt carries `/create:agent`, the typed doctor prompt carries `/doctor:setup`, and both hub prompts report `needsMode` true where the fallback reports none. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/shape-live.jsonl]\nThe in-process top differs from the fallback top on five of the ten corpus prompts: plain readme, plain doctor setup, create-agent, save and repo-rule. On the typed readme prompt the two agree on the command but not on the top skill. The other five agree. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/shape-live.jsonl]\nThe literal rank 6 wiring loses the typed command on the typed readme prompt. The handler names `sk-doc` with `/create:readme`, the existing filter emits a bare `sk-doc` skill row because `commandRowFor('sk-doc')` is empty, and today's fallback keeps `/create:readme`. Feeding handler rows to `degradedRecommendations` with `skillId` as the skill is a regression on the one guarantee Q026 makes. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/shape-live.jsonl]\nThe mode label needs a second small change. The hook's payload mapper already reads `needsMode` from a CLI recommendation, but the Q026 filter's rows carry no such field, so the in-process call alone cannot surface the label. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts:389]\nA whole CLI process that only prints its version costs a median of 33.6 ms and a worst of 39.9 ms, which is the floor any degraded hook call pays before scoring starts. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/cli-startup.jsonl]\nAt its worst the proposed path leaves 2,071.5 ms of the 2,500 ms budget, 82.9 percent; today's worst leaves 1,955.7 ms, 78.2 percent. The proposed path costs 11.2 ms more at the median and 115.8 ms less at the worst case. [INFERENCE: derived from the composed rows in the two files above, each total subtracted from 2,500]\nAt the 10,000-character prompt cap the composed proposed path costs 335.0 ms, 13.4 percent of the budget, and the handler alone costs a cold median of 242.1 ms and a worst of 424.1 ms over five runs, with a warm median of 147.1 ms and a worst of 215.3 ms over ten. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/iteration-003-summary.json]\nThe CLI's module import costs a median of 3.85 ms and a worst of 5.26 ms inside a fresh process. The CLI's own startup is not the budget driver. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/iteration-003-summary.json]\nThe composed proposed path costs a median of 231.1 ms and a worst of 428.5 ms per fresh process, including CLI import, handler import and one scoring call. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/compose-proposed.jsonl]\nThe composed today path costs a median of 219.9 ms and a worst of 544.3 ms on the same prompts, including CLI import and the Python spawn. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/compose-today.jsonl]\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"keyFindings\":17,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolved\":5,\"ruledOut\":8,\"metrics\":{\"iterationsCompleted\":3,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolvedQuestions\":5,\"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\":0,\"keyFindings\":17,\"rejectedPatterns\":0,\"suppressedCandidates\":0,\"observedIdeas\":0,\"promotedIdeas\":0,\"suppressedIdeas\":0,\"convergenceScore\":1,\"coverageBySources\":{\"code\":11,\"other\":12},\"completedPivots\":0,\"failedPivots\":0,\"pivotOverrides\":0}}\nA cold in-process path costs a median of 224.7 ms and a worst of 337.7 ms for import plus one handler call, and the whole fresh process finishes at a median of 239.4 ms and a worst of 443.0 ms. That worst case is 17.7 percent of the hook's 2,500 ms budget. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/iteration-001-summary.json]\nA warm handler call in a process that already loaded the modules costs a median of 76.5 ms and a worst of 93.6 ms across the ten prompts. [SOURCE: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/lineages/inprocess-timing-4/scratch/warm.jsonl]\nThe cold in-process added cost is at parity with today's fallback, 224.7 ms against 238.5 ms, a difference of minus 13.8 ms at the median. The warm in-process call is 3.1 times cheaper than the Python spawn. A CLI process that already loaded its own modules pays the import only on its first degraded call, so the comparable number is the cold one. [INFERENCE: based on the cold, warm and fallback rows in the same summary file and the dynamic-import shape of the proposed call 